::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A pack of 5 notebooks costs $15. What is the unit price?
{
=A. $3 per notebook#Correct. Divide $15 by 5 notebooks to get $3 per notebook.
~B. $10 per notebook#This subtracts and does not find unit price.
~C. $20 per notebook#This adds dollars and notebooks.
~D. $75 per notebook#This multiplies total cost by number of notebooks.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.1
Question:
What is 20% of $60?
{
~A. $6#This is 10% of $60, not 20%.
=B. $12#Correct. 0.20 x 60 = 12.
~C. $20#This uses the percent number as dollars.
~D. $80#This adds 20 to 60.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.4
Question:
Use d = rt to solve for r.
{
~A. r = dt#This multiplies instead of dividing.
~B. r = t/d#This reverses the quotient.
=C. r = d/t#Correct. Divide distance by time to isolate rate.
~D. r = d + t#This adds quantities.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.5
Question:
If C(h) = 10h + 25, what is C(4)?
{
~A. 39#This adds 10, 4, and 25 instead of multiplying.
~B. 40#This omits the fixed 25.
~C. 250#This multiplies 10 and 25.
=D. 65#Correct. C(4) = 10(4) + 25 = 65.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.6
Question:
A value changes from 20 to 50 as time changes from 2 to 8. What is the average rate of change?
{
=A. 5#Correct. Output changes by 30 and input changes by 6, so 30/6 = 5.
~B. 30#This is the output change only.
~C. 6#This is the input change only.
~D. 70#This adds the output values.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Find the weighted score for 80 at 25% and 100 at 75%.
{
~A. 90#This is the simple average.
=B. 95#Correct. 80(0.25) + 100(0.75) = 20 + 75 = 95.
~C. 180#This adds scores without weights.
~D. 75#This omits the first weighted product.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.MC.3
Question:
Why is 49 x 4 about 200 reasonable?
{
~A. 49 + 4 is close to 200#This uses addition.
~B. 200 is reasonable because it is large#Size alone does not justify reasonableness.
=C. 49 is close to 50, and 50 x 4 = 200#Correct. Rounding supports the estimate.
~D. Estimates cannot check products#Estimates can check multiplication.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.1
Question:
A price decreases from $80 to $60. What is the percent decrease?
{
~A. 20%#This uses the dollar change as a percent without dividing by original.
~B. 33.3%#This compares change to the new price.
~C. 75%#This is the new price as a percent of original.
=D. 25%#Correct. The decrease is $20, and 20/80 = 25%.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
Which ratio is equivalent to 2 to 7?
{
=A. 4 to 14#Correct. Both parts are multiplied by 2.
~B. 2 to 14#Only one part changed.
~C. 4 to 7#Only one part changed.
~D. 9 to 14#Adding the same number does not preserve the ratio.
}
::MCR_U01_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U01_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: Unit 1 standards synthesis
Question:
Which process best supports real-world quantitative reasoning?
{
~A. Guess from the largest number#This ignores context and units.
=B. Identify quantities, choose a tool, solve, and check reasonableness#Correct. This matches the Unit 1 reasoning process.
~C. Remove units before solving#Units are needed for interpretation.
~D. Use the same operation for every problem#Different contexts need different tools.
}
